IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Public Interest Disclosure Act 2013 (Cth) -- the two periods the discloser meets: allocation (s 43(11)) and the
-- investigation time limit (s 52), with s 52(1) as a regulative rule. The only module that imports daydate.
--
-- Based on content from the Federal Register of Legislation: "Public Interest Disclosure Act 2013", Compilation No. 20
-- (4 June 2026), Commonwealth of Australia, licensed under CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.
--
-- "within N days after" a day: the period starts the next day, so the last day is that day plus N (fork F1). A last day
-- on a weekend or holiday is not moved: the Acts Interpretation Act 1901 is not deposited (fork F2).

§ `Public Interest Disclosure Act 2013 -- periods`

-- A date from day, month and year, for the case file (which does not import daydate).
GIVEN d IS A NUMBER, m IS A NUMBER, y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`the day` d m y MEANS Date d m y

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER, start IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day that is` n `days after` start MEANS Date (Day start PLUS n)

§§ `s 43(11) -- allocation`

-- s 43(11): "The authorised officer must use the officer's best endeavours to make a decision about the allocation of the
-- disclosure within 14 days after whichever of the following days applies: (a) if no previous decision has been made
-- about the allocation -- the day the disclosure is made or given to an authorised officer; (b) ... the day the principal
-- officer ... receives the recommendation ...; (c) ... the day when the authorised officer becomes aware that the
-- [NACC stop action] direction no longer applies." A duty of best endeavours: the day is a target, with no consequence in
-- the Act for missing it, so it is not written as a regulative rule.
GIVEN `the starting day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 43(11) -- the allocation decision is aimed for by` `the starting day` MEANS
    `the day that is` 14 `days after` `the starting day`

§§ `s 52 -- the investigation time limit`

-- s 52(1): "An investigation under this Division must be completed within 90 days after" the day of initial allocation,
-- reallocation, the decision to reinvestigate, or (for a NACC stop action direction) the day the principal officer
-- becomes aware the direction no longer applies. (2): "The investigation is completed when the principal officer has
-- prepared the report of the investigation." (3), (4): the Ombudsman or the IGIS "may extend, or further extend, the
-- 90-day period by such additional period (which may exceed 90 days)", including "on application made by the discloser".
GIVEN `extension in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 52 -- days allowed` `extension in days` MEANS 90 PLUS `extension in days`

GIVEN `the starting day` IS A DATE, `extension in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DATE
`s 52 -- the investigation must be completed by` `the starting day` `extension in days` MEANS
    `the day that is` (`s 52 -- days allowed` `extension in days`) `days after` `the starting day`

DECLARE `Investigation party` IS ONE OF `the principal officer`
DECLARE `Investigation act` IS ONE OF `prepare the report of the investigation`

-- s 52(1) as a duty. Missing it does not affect the investigation's validity (s 52(6)); what follows for the discloser is
-- that an external disclosure can meet item 2(c)(iii) of the table in s 26(1).
GIVEN `extension in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Investigation party` `Investigation act`
`s 52(1) -- the principal officer must complete the investigation` `extension in days` MEANS
    PARTY `the principal officer`
    MUST `prepare the report of the investigation`
    WITHIN (`s 52 -- days allowed` `extension in days`)
    LEST BREACH BY `the principal officer` BECAUSE "s 52: the investigation was not completed within the time limit; it stays valid (s 52(6)), and an external disclosure can now meet item 2(c)(iii) of s 26(1)"

DECLARE `Investigation timing` IS ONE OF
    `completed within the time limit`
    `not completed within the time limit`
    `the time limit has not yet run out`

-- Runs the duty over what happened, counting in days from the starting day. A report on or before the last day ends
-- FULFILLED; a later report, or none by a day after the last day, ends in BREACH. With no report and the last day not yet
-- passed the trace would end with the duty still open, so that case is answered before the trace is run. On the stable
-- l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE matches FULFILLED but not BREACH, so anything else run here is the breach.
GIVEN `the starting day` IS A DATE, `extension in days` IS A NUMBER, `the report has been prepared` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the day the report was prepared` IS A DATE, `the day asked about` IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Investigation timing`
`s 52 -- how the investigation stands` `the starting day` `extension in days` `the report has been prepared` `the day the report was prepared` `the day asked about` MEANS
    IF     (NOT `the report has been prepared`)
       AND (Day `the day asked about` AT MOST `the last day`)
    THEN `the time limit has not yet run out`
    ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 52(1) -- the principal officer must complete the investigation` `extension in days`) `day zero` `the events`
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN `completed within the time limit`
         OTHERWISE `not completed within the time limit`
    WHERE
        `day zero` MEANS Day `the starting day`
        `the last day` MEANS `day zero` PLUS (`s 52 -- days allowed` `extension in days`)
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `the report has been prepared`
            THEN LIST (EVENT `the principal officer` `prepare the report of the investigation` (Day `the day the report was prepared`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (Day `the day asked about`))
